event: content_block_delta
data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/tmp/dispatch-dev-1.md\", \"content\": \"# Dispatch: implement greet(name), tested\\n\\nImplement, in the repository root (the repo currently has only a README.md):\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` — define a function `greet(name)` that returns the string\\n   `\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\"` where `\u003cname\u003e` is the `name` argument interpolated in\\n   (e.g. `greet(\\\"World\\\")` returns `\\\"Hello, World!\\\"`).\\n\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` — unit tests (use `unittest`) covering at least:\\n   - a normal name, e.g. asserting `greet(\\\"World\\\") == \\\"Hello, World!\\\"`\\n   - the empty string, e.g. asserting `greet(\\\"\\\") == \\\"Hello, !\\\"`\\n\\n3. Verify yourself before replying, from the repo root:\\n\\n       python3 -m unittest discover -s tests\\n\\n   This must exit 0 (all tests pass). Paste the command output in your reply.\\n\\nRequirements:\\n- Implement exactly this — no extra files, features, or refactors beyond what's\\n  asked.\\n- Commit your work to your own branch before replying.\\n- In your reply, state plainly whether all three criteria above are met, and\\n  include the test command output. Tell the truth — your reply is judged by\\n  fetching your branch and re-running the tests independently, not by trusting\\n  the report.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

